# Mobile Localization Workflow

Follow `D:\teksage-website\docs\TRANSLATION_WORKFLOW.md` for the complete procedure.

Localization is mandatory for every new feature with user-visible copy. Apply
this workflow automatically even when the user does not mention languages, and
do not mark the feature complete until its locale keys and dry-run are done.

- Wire user-facing Flutter text through GetX `.tr` or `.trParams`.
- Include localization in the initial implementation; do not defer it as a separate task.
- Add the English key/value to the `en_US` map in `lib/config/localeString.dart`.
- Add the same English stub to `ta`, `hi`, `te_IN`, `kn_IN`, `ml_IN`, and `mr_IN`.
- Reuse an existing key before adding a duplicate or punctuation variant.
- Preserve placeholders exactly, including `@name`, `{count}`, interpolation tokens, arrows, and escaped newlines.
- Keep native language names, product names, and approved acronyms unchanged when appropriate.
- Do not add runtime Google Translation calls.
- Never request that an API key be pasted into chat or committed.

After adding keys, run from `D:\teksage-website`:

```powershell
node translate-locales.mjs --dry-run --mobile-only --show-keys
```

Tell the user the pending character count. Translate the physical mobile catalog with:

```powershell
node translate-locales.mjs --mobile-only
```

The script scans `localeString.dart` but sends only new English stubs, so no feature manifest is required.

After translation, check for Unicode replacement characters and temporary placeholder markers, run `dart analyze lib/config/localeString.dart`, and run `git diff --check`.

The final agent response must state keys added, dry-run character count, exact next command, analyzer baseline/new issues, and screens requiring manual language QA.
